*a plain deal table in there & some wicker armchairs which Jorgenson had produced from somewhere in the depths of the ship.* The alteration of *were* to *was* would now be an improvement; but why, if *were* was best in the bare framework
>>>

<<<
attached to chairs & not to table a long relative clause; third & most important, he has had, in order to cut off the relative clause from table, to shift in there to an earlier place.
===
attached to *chairs* & not to *table* a long relative clause; third & most important, he has had, in order to cut off the relative clause from *table,* to shift *in there* to an earlier place.
>>>

<<<
But it results that the verbal phrase there were . . . in there) is so arranged
